Consider this topology:
-----------------------------------
|        192.168.1.0/24           |
|                                 |       192.168.12.0/24
| PC1 .1 ----- SW1 ---------.254 R1 .1 --------------------- .2 R2
|(1111)Gi0/0 Gi0/1  Gi0/0    Gi0/2 .1 Gi0/1                 Gi0/0 .2
|                           (aaaa)    (bbbb)             (cccc) |
| ----------------------------- Gi0/1                         Gi0/1
                                  |                           (dddd)
                                  |                             |
                                  |                             |
                  192.168.13.0/24 |                             |  192.168.24.0/24
                                  |                             |
                                  |                             |
                                  |                            Gi0/1 ----------------------------------
                                Gi0/1                         (eeee)                                  |
                                  .3                            .4          192.168.4.0/24            |
                                  R3 .3 -------------------- .4 R4 .254 --------- SW4 -------- .1 PC4 |
                                    Gi0/0  192.168.34.0/24 Gi0/0 | Gi0/2    Gi0/0    Gi0/1 Gi0/0(4444)|
                                                                 |(fffe)                              |
                                                                 |                                    |
                                                                 --------------------------------------

Assume we configured the static routes so that a packet goes from PC1 to PC4 using R2. MAC addresses were only added to the path we will follow. So let's say PC1
wants to send data to PC4, here would be the steps:

1. Src IP: 192.168.1.1 and Dst IP: 192.168.4.1. PC1 realises that Dst IP is in a different network, so it needs to send it to its default gateway first. But it doesn't 
know its gateway yet, so it uses ARP.

2. PC1 makes this ARP request packet: Src IP: 192.168.1.1, Dst IP: 192.168.1.254, Dst MAC: ffff, Src MAC: 1111

3. SW1 receives the frame, and broadcast it to all its interfaces except the one it came from. SW1 will learn PC1 MAC address this way.

4. R1 receives the frame and notices that the Dst IP is its IP, so it sends an ARP Reply with its MAC address to PC1: Src IP: 192.168.1.254, Dst IP: 192.168.1.1,
Dst MAC: 1111, Src MAC: aaaa

5. SW1 receives the ARP reply and sends it to PC1. It will learn R1 MAC address this way.

6. PC1 sends a packet to R1 with the following: Src IP: 192.168.1.1 and Dst IP: 192.168.4.1, Dst MAC: aaaa, Src MAC: 1111.

7. R1 receives the packet and check its routing table. It sees that the next hop for 192.168.4.0/24 is 192.168.12.2, but it doesnt know the MAC address, so it will
use ARP.

8. R1 makes this ARP request packet: Src IP: 192.168.12.1 and Dst IP: 192.168.12.2, Dst MAC: ffff, Src MAC: bbbb

9. R2 receives the frame and notices that the Dst IP is its IP, so it sends an ARP Reply with its MAC address to PC1: Src IP: 192.168.12.2, Dst IP: 192.168.12.1,
Dst MAC: bbbb, Src MAC: cccc

10. R1 sends a packet to R2 with the following: Src IP: 192.168.1.1 and Dst IP: 192.168.4.1, Dst MAC: cccc, Src MAC: bbbb.

11. R2 receives the packet and check its routing table. It sees that the next hop for 192.168.4.0/24 is 192.168.24.4, but it doesnt know the MAC address, so it will
use ARP.

12. R2 makes this ARP request packet: Src IP: 192.168.24.2 and Dst IP: 192.168.24.4, Dst MAC: ffff, Src MAC: dddd

13. R4 receives the frame and notices that the Dst IP is its IP, so it sends an ARP Reply with its MAC address to PC1: Src IP: 192.168.24.4, Dst IP: 192.168.24.2,
Dst MAC: dddd, Src MAC: eeee

14. R2 sends a packet to R4 with the following: Src IP: 192.168.1.1 and Dst IP: 192.168.4.1, Dst MAC: eeee, Src MAC: dddd.

15. R4 receives the packet and check its routing table. It sees that the next hop for 192.168.4.0/24 is directly connected in its Gi0/2, but it doesnt know the MAC
address, so it will use ARP.

16. R4 makes this ARP request packet: Src IP: 192.168.4.254 and Dst IP: 192.168.4.1, Dst MAC: ffff, Src MAC: fffe

17. SW4 receives the frame, and broadcast it to all its interfaces except the one it came from. SW4 will learn R4 MAC address this way.

18. PC4 receives the frame and notices that the Dst IP is its IP, so it sends an ARP Reply with its MAC address to R4: Src IP: 192.168.4.1, Dst IP: 192.168.4.254,
Dst MAC: fffe, Src MAC: 4444

19. SW4 receives the ARP reply and sends it to R4. It will learn PC4 MAC address this way.

20. R4 sends a packet to PC4 with the following: Src IP: 192.168.1.1 and Dst IP: 192.168.4.1, Dst MAC: 4444, Src MAC: fffe.

21. If PC4 needs to send data back to PC1, it will do the inverse route, except no need for ARP requests and reply since the devices know the different MAC 
addresses now. They only have to forward the packet and encapsulated/deencapsulate with the correct MAC addresses.
